<h2>The application</h2>
<p>The applicant is an accused in FIR No. 137/2026 of PS Chanakyapuri under Section 21 of the NDPS Act, with the sessions case pending before the Special Judge (NDPS), Rouse Avenue Courts. Her husband died on 20 November 2026. The next day, she moved the High Court under Section 528 of the BNSS, 2023 (the successor to Section 482 CrPC) seeking interim bail to attend his last rites and the ceremonies that follow.</p>

<h2>Verification first, leniency after</h2>
<p>Before anything else, the Court had the ground verified: the sub-inspector appearing for the investigating officer and the Additional Public Prosecutor both confirmed the death. With the foundation confirmed and the State not disputing the facts, Justice Mini Pushkarna took what the order describes as a lenient view and granted interim bail for two weeks from the date of the order.</p>

<h2>The conditions</h2>
<div class="check">
  <ul>
    <li>Personal bond of Rs 25,000 with one surety in the like amount, to the satisfaction of the Jail Superintendent;</li>
    <li>appearance before the trial court as and when directed;</li>
    <li>no criminal activity of any kind during the period;</li>
    <li>residential address and mobile number to be furnished to the investigating officer and the SHO, the phone to be kept operational, and neither to be changed without intimation;</li>
    <li>no inducement, threat or promise to any person acquainted with the facts, no approach to prosecution witnesses, and no tampering with evidence;</li>
    <li>no travel outside the NCT of Delhi without intimation to the investigating officer; and</li>
    <li>surrender before the Jail Superintendent by 4:00 PM on the expiry of two weeks, with cancellation available on a report if any condition is breached.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2>How humanitarian interim bail fits into bail law</h2>
<p>Interim bail of this kind is not a comment on the merits of the case; it is the system&rsquo;s recognition that incarceration before conviction should not strip a person of participation in the most basic events of family life where that can be managed safely. Three features of this order repay attention. First, the speed: the death occurred on 20 November and the High Court order came on 21 November — these applications are heard urgently, and families should move immediately with the death certificate or hospital record. Second, the role of the prosecution: confirmation by the investigating officer transformed the hearing, and practitioners should request verification through the IO in advance of the listing wherever possible. Third, the structure of the relief: a fixed period, a fixed surrender time, and conditions calibrated to the specific risks the State might otherwise argue — flight, witness contact and reoffending.</p>
<p>The contrast with abuse of the remedy is sharp. The same week, in another matter before the same Court, an interim bail application was dismissed outright where the supporting affidavit was found to be false. Humanitarian relief rests entirely on the court&rsquo;s trust in the stated ground; that trust, once verified, produces quick and effective orders, and once betrayed, closes the door.</p>
<div class="note">Interim bail is a creature of judicial practice rather than a separately defined statutory category; it is granted pending consideration of bail applications under Sections 480 to 483 of the BNSS, 2023 (formerly Sections 437 to 439 CrPC). Custody parole — escorted release for a few hours — is the narrower alternative courts use where even short unsupervised release is considered unsafe. Which of the two is appropriate depends on the case record.</div>
